[41.819419, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[41.926363, "o", "Help on class DynamicSink in module bytewax.outputs:\r\n\r\nclass DynamicSink(Sink)\r\n |  An output sink where all workers write items concurrently.\r\n |\r\n |  Does not support storing any resume state. Thus these kind of\r\n |  outputs only naively can support at-least-once processing.\r\n |\r\n |  Method resolution order:\r\n |      DynamicSink\r\n |      Sink\r\n |      abc.ABC\r\n |      typing.Generic\r\n |      builtins.object\r\n |\r\n |  Methods defined here:\r\n |\r\n |  build(self, step_id: str, worker_index: int, worker_count: int) -> bytewax.outputs.StatelessSinkPartition[~X]\r\n |      Build an output partition for a worker.\r\n |\r\n |      Will be called once on each worker.\r\n |\r\n |      :arg step_id: The step_id of the output operator.\r\n |\r\n |      :arg worker_index: Index of this worker.\r\n |\r\n |      :arg worker_count: Total number of workers.\r\n |\r\n |      :returns: The built partition.\r\n |\r\n |  ----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n |  Data and other attributes defined here:\r\n |\r\n |  __abstractmethods__ = frozenset({'build'})\r\n |\r\n |  __annotations__ = {}\r\n |\r\n |  __orig_bases__ = (bytewax.outputs.Sink[~X],)\r\n |\r\n |  __parameters__ = (~X,)\r\n |\r\n |  ----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n |  Data descriptors inherited from Sink:\r\n |\r\n |  __dict__\r\n |      dictionary for instance variables\r\n |\r\n |  __weakref__\r\n |      list of weak references to the object\r\n |\r\n"]
[41.938606, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@18d2f3f7-b4a2-412c-b9af-2792ebfb4d90:~# "]
[49.714527, "i", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.output)\" 2>&1 | head -30\n"]
[49.715053, "o", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.output)\" 2>&1 | head -30\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[49.802328, "o", "Help on function output in module bytewax.operators:\r\n\r\noutput(step_id: str, up: bytewax.dataflow.Stream[~X], sink: bytewax.outputs.Sink[~X]) -> None\r\n    Write items out of a dataflow.\r\n\r\n    ```{testcode}\r\n    from bytewax.dataflow import Dataflow\r\n    import bytewax.operators as op\r\n    from bytewax.testing import TestingSource\r\n    from bytewax.connectors.stdio import StdOutSink\r\n\r\n    flow = Dataflow(\"output_eg\")\r\n    nums = op.input(\"nums\", flow, TestingSource([1, 2, 3]))\r\n\r\n    # This will print the items to stdout.\r\n    # You can replace this with any other sink.\r\n    op.output(\"sink\", nums, StdOutSink())\r\n    ```\r\n"]
[49.802469, "o", "\r\n    ```{testcode}\r\n    :hide:\r\n\r\n    from bytewax.testing import run_main\r\n\r\n    run_main(flow)\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n    ```{testoutput}\r\n    1\r\n    2\r\n"]
[49.812821, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@18d2f3f7-b4a2-412c-b9af-2792ebfb4d90:~# "]
[50.680249, "i", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.key_on)\" 2>&1 | head -20\n"]
[50.680827, "o", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.key_on)\" 2>&1 | head -20\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[50.773093, "o", "Help on function key_on in module bytewax.operators:\r\n\r\nkey_on(step_id: str, up: bytewax.dataflow.Stream[~X], key: Callable[[~X], str]) -> bytewax.dataflow.Stream[typing.Tuple[str, ~X]]\r\n    Add a key for each item.\r\n\r\n    This allows you to use all the keyed operators that require the\r\n    upstream to be a {py:obj}`KeyedStream`.\r\n\r\n    ```{testcode}\r\n    from bytewax.dataflow import Dataflow\r\n    import bytewax.operators as op\r\n    from bytewax.testing import TestingSource\r\n    from datetime import timedelta\r\n\r\n    flow = Dataflow(\"collect_eg\")\r\n    source = [\r\n        {\"key\": \"a\", \"val\": 1},\r\n        {\"key\": \"b\", \"val\": 2}\r\n    ]\r\n    nums = op.input(\"nums\", flow, TestingSource(source))\r\n"]
[50.783686, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@18d2f3f7-b4a2-412c-b9af-2792ebfb4d90:~# "]
[51.697349, "i", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.filter)\" 2>&1 | head -20\n"]
[51.697834, "o", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.filter)\" 2>&1 | head -20\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[51.795226, "o", "Help on function filter in module bytewax.operators:\r\n\r\nfilter(step_id: str, up: bytewax.dataflow.Stream[~X], predicate: Callable[[~X], bool]) -> bytewax.dataflow.Stream[~X]\r\n    Keep only some items.\r\n\r\n    It is commonly used for:\r\n\r\n    - Selecting relevant events\r\n\r\n    - Removing empty events\r\n\r\n    - Removing sentinels\r\n\r\n    - Removing stop words\r\n\r\n    ```{testcode}\r\n    import bytewax.operators as op\r\n    from bytewax.testing import TestingSource\r\n    from bytewax.dataflow import Dataflow\r\n\r\n"]
[51.805698, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@18d2f3f7-b4a2-412c-b9af-2792ebfb4d90:~# "]
[52.731445, "i", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.input)\" 2>&1 | head -20\n"]
[52.732221, "o", "python3 -c \"import bytewax.operators as op; help(op.input)\" 2>&1 | head -20\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[52.817783, "o", "Help on function input in module bytewax.operators:\r\n\r\ninput(step_id: str, flow: bytewax.dataflow.Dataflow, source: bytewax.inputs.Source[~X]) -> bytewax.dataflow.Stream[~X]\r\n    Introduce items into a dataflow.\r\n\r\n    ```{testcode}\r\n    from bytewax.dataflow import Dataflow\r\n    import bytewax.operators as op\r\n"]
[52.81798, "o", "    from bytewax.testing import TestingSource\r\n\r\n    flow = Dataflow(\"input_eg\")\r\n    nums = op.input(\"nums\", flow, TestingSource([10, 20, 30]))\r\n    op.inspect(\"out\", nums)\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n    ```{testcode}\r\n    :hide:\r\n    from bytewax.testing import run_main\r\n\r\n    run_main(flow)\r\n"]
[52.82784, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@18d2f3f7-b4a2-412c-b9af-2792ebfb4d90:~# "]
[80.215873, "i", "cat > /home/user/bytewax_batching/dataflow.py << 'PYEOF'\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sqlite3\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nfrom bytewax.connectors.files import FileSource\nfrom bytewax.dataflow import Dataflow\nfrom bytewax.operators import DynamicSink, StatelessSinkPartition\nimport bytewax.operators as op\n\n\n# Read run-id\nRUN_ID_FILE = \"/logs/artifacts/run-id\"\nwith open(RUN_ID_FILE, \"r\") as f:\n    RUN_ID = f.read().strip()\n\nDB_PATH = f\"/home/user/bytewax_batching/metrics-{RUN_ID}.db\"\nINPUT_PATH = \"/home/user/bytewax_batching/input.jsonl\"\nOUTPUT_LOG = \"/home/user/bytewax_batching/output.log\"\n\n\ndef ensure_table():\n    \"\"\"Create the SQLite database and the device_metrics table if it doesn't exist.\"\"\"\n    conn = sqlite3.connect(DB_PATH)\n    try:\n        conn.execute(\n            \"\"\"\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS device_metrics (\n                device_id TEXT,\n                timestamp TEXT,\n                metric_value REAL\n            )\n            \"\"\"\n        )\n        conn.commit()\n    finally:\n        conn.close()\n\n\nclass SQLiteSink(DynamicSink):\n    \"\"\"Custom Bytewax Sink that bulk-inserts batches into SQLite using executemany.\"\"\"\n\n    def build(self, step_id, worker_index, worker_count):\n        return _SQLiteSinkPartition()\n\n\nclass _SQLiteSinkPartition(StatelessSinkPartition):\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._conn = sqlite3.connect(DB_PATH)\n\n    def write_batch(self, items):\n        # `items` is the list of batches from op.collect; each item is a\n        # (key, [dict, dict, ...]) tuple produced by op.collect.\n        rows = []\n        for _key, batch in items:\n            for record in batch:\n                rows.append(\n                    (\n                        record[\"device_id\"],\n                        record[\"timestamp\"],\n                        record[\"metric_value\"],\n                    )\n                )\n        if rows:\n            self._conn.executemany(\n                \"INSERT INTO device_metrics (device_id, timestamp, metric_value) \"\n                \"VALUES (?, ?, ?)\",\n                rows,\n            )\n            self._conn.commit()\n\n    def close(self):\n        try:\n            self._conn.commit()\n        finally:\n            self._conn.close()\n\n\ndef parse_line(line: str):\n    \"\"\"Parse a single JSONL line into a metric dict.\"\"\"\n    line = line.strip()\n    if not line:\n        return None\n    return json.loads(line)\n\n\ndef main():\n    # Ensure the SQLite table exists before the dataflow starts writing.\n    ensure_table()\n\n    flow = Dataflow(\"device_metrics_batching\")\n\n    # Read device metrics from the JSONL file.\n    source = FileSource(INPUT_PATH)\n    metrics = op.input(\"metrics_in\", flow, source)\n\n    # Parse the JSON string into a dict.\n    parsed = op.map(\"parse\", metrics, parse_line)\n\n    # Filter out any metrics where metric_value is less than 0.\n    filtered = op.filter(\"non_negative\", parsed, lambda r: r is not None and r[\"metric_value\"] >= 0)\n\n    # Group the metrics by device_id.\n    keyed = op.key_on(\"by_device\", filtered, lambda r: r[\"device_id\"])\n\n    # Batch the metrics for each device using max batch size of 10 and timeout of 1 second.\n    batched = op.collect(\n        \"batch\",\n        keyed,\n        timeout=timedelta(seconds=1),\n        max_size=10,\n    )\n\n    # Write batched metrics to SQLite using a custom DynamicSink.\n    op.output(\"sqlite_sink\", batched, SQLiteSink())\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n    # After the dataflow completes successfully, write the log message.\n    with open(OUTPUT_LOG, \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(\"Pipeline finished.\\n\")\nPYEOF\n"]
